Screening result: === SCREENING TASK: Study 95 ===

SELECTION CRITERIA:
Randomised or quasi‐randomised controlled trials in women with SUI, UUI or MUI (based on symptoms, signs or 
urodynamics). One arm of the trial included PFMT. Another arm was a no treatment, placebo, sham or other inactive 
control treatment arm.

STUDY TITLE:
Pelvic floor muscle training versus other active treatments for urinary incontinence in women

STUDY ABSTRACT:
Urinary incontinence is a common problem in adult women. The true scope of the problem is difficult to estimate and is 
influenced by the definition of incontinence used and the sample population studied. Previous analysis of urinary 
incontinence data found prevalence rates amongst European women ranged from 14.1% to 68.8%(Cerruto 2013). Data from a 
large scale multi‐national (Canada, Germany, Italy, Sweden and the United Kingdom) cross‐sectional study of over 19,000 
participants found an overall prevalence of all types of urinary incontinence in women to be 13.1% and showed an 
increasing prevalence with age (Irwin 2006). However, figures are unlikely to reflect the true prevalence of the problem
due to embarrassment and other factors which lead to significant under‐reporting.Urinary incontinence causes significant
morbidity. Women are stigmatised by their condition and often find difficulty in maintaining employment and performing 
the activities of daily living. Not only are the symptoms troublesome but also extremely upsetting and can have a 
profound psychological as well as a social, sexual and hygienic impact. Many patients adopt elaborate coping mechanisms 
including voiding frequently, mapping out the location of toilets, drinking less or wearing dark clothing to mask 
incontinence episodes. Others resort to wearing incontinence pads or sanitary towels.cochranelibrary.com

INSTRUCTIONS: Based on the selection criteria above, decide whether this study should be INCLUDED or EXCLUDED. Provide a
brief rationale tied to specific criteria.


